// ============================================================
// CHARACTER: Simon Peter (Denial)
// ACT: Act III
// CASE: The Last Supper
// CASE ID: last_supper
// SOURCE: ORPHAN FILE - Peter's denial before the crucifixion
// BIBLE REFERENCE: John 18:15-27; Luke 22:56-62
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PROPHECIES FULFILLED IN THIS CASE:
//   - Psalm 31:22
//     Gospel: Matthew 26:71-72 — Peter's denial
//     Insight: "I said in my alarm, I am cut off from your sight." Peter felt abandoned but was not truly lost.
//   - Isaiah 53:10
//     Gospel: The suffering servant's apparent abandonment
//     Insight: Peter's denial was part of the larger pattern of apparent abandonment that led to resurrection victory.
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// BIBLICAL CONTEXT:
//   Summary: Before the crucifixion, Peter denied knowing Jesus three times. He stood in the high priest's courtyard and denied being His disciple.
//   Historical Note: The rooster's crow was a fulfillment of Jesus's prediction. Peter went out and wept bitterly.
//   Significance: Peter's denial shows that even the boldest of disciples can fall — but failure is not final.
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// CASE FACTS (Truth Object):
//   culprit: fear (Peter's denial was driven by fear of arrest)
//   motive: Self-preservation over loyalty
//   method: Three denials — "I do not know the man," "I do not know him," and cursing with oaths
// ============================================================
//

-> start
=== start ===
I am Simon Peter. Three times I denied the man I swore I would never deny. The rooster crowed, and He turned. I saw the look in His eyes — disappointment, not anger. I am a broken man.
* [What did you say?] -> denials
* [The look — what did it mean?] -> look
=== denials ===
First, by the fire. 'Woman, I do not know him.' Then again — 'I do not know the man.' The third time, I cursed and swore I was not one of His disciples. Each denial carved deeper than the last.
* [And then?] -> look
=== look ===
He turned. Not with rage — never with rage. But His eyes... they held all the love I had broken. I saw my own weakness reflected there. I went out and wept. That night, I thought I was finished.
* [Peter...] -> closing
=== closing ===
I am here. I do not know why. Perhaps grace is too heavy for me to understand. The other disciples avoided me. My calling felt burned away with those words. Say what you will — but I was broken.
-> DONE
